hi i'm elizabeth a medical student  living in london and in this video i  wanted to talk about the  experience and overcoming impostor  syndrome in medicine  this will probably be quite a rambly  video so i do apologize i will try to  make it as structured and valuable as  possible but  yeah just so you know this is the  question i get asked the most  i did go from experiencing quite bad  imposter syndrome when i first started  medicine  to basically having none at all in the  last two years and i think i've been  very very  fortunate in the people i've been  surrounded with the things i've  experienced  and all of that has helped change and  shape the way that i view things  so hopefully through recounting my  experiences and my thoughts  you might be able to overcome imposter  syndrome in yourself  and therefore be more able to enjoy life  so that is my goal here  i really really hope this helps first of  all i think it's very important to kind  of define what imposter syndrome is  exactly  i'm talking about those of us who enjoy  being around  patients enjoy everything that medicine  is about  but suffer because we think that we are  not good enough  and for me in the beginning it was very  much the thought  of oh my god i've come here by accident  um this was an error and they're going  to realize at some point that i'm not as  smart as everyone else  i'm not as organized as everyone else i  am not as capable as everyone else  i'm going to fail all my exams and then  i'm going to leave and this is going to  be the most humiliating thing  so when you have pleasure on one side  with enjoying the course and that  crippling fear and anxiety on the other  side  feeling as though there's everyone else  who is doing fine  and then there is you i think this is  the way that i saw imposter syndrome and  how i would define my early experiences  jumping straight into it i think that  one thing that helped me  is realizing that it would actually be  unreasonable  to not have doubts about medicine and to  not have doubts about your intelligence  and your performance  because realistically if you haven't  started medicine yet  you have no evidence and no proof that  you will be good at it you don't even  know if you're going to be good at  university  you don't know if it's right for you of  course because you haven't done it  and to have doubts around that i think  it's absolutely normal and it's healthy  and it's fine  i think it would be unreasonable that  without going into university or without  starting yet to think that  this is going to be perfect i'm going to  be the best doctor out there i think  that would be unreasonable i think  doubts and uncertainty within themselves  are not negative it just depends on how  you interpret uncertainty itself  and the feelings that surround it those  can be positive or  negative i think that embracing  uncertainty is something that will  become more and more necessary as you  get older  and therefore as we grow older we do  become better  at it one way that really helped me see  things differently  is to think that okay i feel anxious now  because i'm not sure and i don't have  enough evidence that i'm going to be  good at this  when will i have that so will it be when  i've done my first lecture will it be  when i've sat my first exam  will it be when i've seen my first  patient when i've gone to dissection  well is  when when will i know that i can  actually be a doctor none of these  points if you just go on and on you will  see that you will never reach a point  where you think okay this is sufficient  evidence to know that i will pass  or that i will be good because even if  you pass the first year you're just  going to start all over again in the  second year  and the third and the fourth and  actually you're never going to know  what it's like being a doctor until you  are a doctor which is five or six years  down the line  and even then you will only know what  it's like to be a junior doctor you  won't know  what it's like to be a reg or a  consultant i think  realizing that there will be uncertainty  along every single step of the way  and no matter how much information you  are given you will never know  for a hundred percent that you are right  for this or that this is right for you  but you just do the best with what is  there so i think that especially when  you're just starting  and you're really really worried about  oh my god i i don't know how this will  go  just realizing that you know what it's  fine and that's probably never going to  go away  and there's always going to be more  things that i don't know about my  suitability  than i do so it's really not worth  stressing about it  at all while one aspect of imposter  syndrome is around your own ability and  capabilities another huge part of it has  to do with how you compare to others  and i think personally this was one of  the most difficult ones for me  because it's a relatively competitive  and difficult  degree to get into so chances are  you're quite competitive and so is  everyone else for example  for me it felt as though everyone was  the child of a doctor  it felt as though i was surrounded by  people whose grandparents had nobel  prizes in medicine  and i don't come from a medical  background and the most exposure i had  to medicine apart from my placement was  going to get a vaccine because  fortunately until that point i've been  very healthy  so it felt as though i was starting on  the wrong foot and these people  all had an upper hand and they were  going to do so much better than me now  i think that competitiveness is fine  if it's positive it can push you forward  to want to do more things which i guess  is okay  but negative competitiveness is one of  the worst feelings  and i think that you should not create a  competition for yourself  in something that you cannot compete in  you cannot change  i cannot change my parents professions i  cannot change what my grandparents did  i cannot change my past and how much  exposure i had to medicine  these are all things that i have no  power over so  it's not a competition it doesn't matter  i should not pay attention to these  things at  all i think realizing the difference  between things that you can control and  things that you cannot control  is very very important for example i've  always worked full time alongside my  medical degree which means that i  couldn't go to the library as much or  attend the extra talks that my friends  went to and  although all of these would make me  panic in the beginning i just realized  that  it's just something that i cannot change  um i will do the best where i can  but i'm not going to worry about this  there's no point in me feeling as though  i am less than  because they can do all these other  things there's definitely advantages  to whatever you're doing and whatever  position you are in this advantage might  be that your great grandfather was a  doctor but your other advantage might be  that because you work so much  outside of medicine you have better  communication with your patients  or you pay more attention when you are  studying there are sides to every single  thing  i wouldn't take it for granted that  people are somehow better than you  or more experienced than you just  because of how things seem in the  beginning  and in general i think the way that i  see things in almost everything in life  is  what i can change and what i cannot  change if i cannot change something  there's literally no point in me paying  any attention to it at all  and especially if that thing that i  cannot change  causes only negative feelings for me i  think that's absolutely ridiculous  if there's something that i cannot  change that for some reason makes me  extremely happy  that's fine but if it's something that i  cannot change and keeps me up at night  because i think i'm going to fail  or because i think that i'm just so much  worse than everyone else  then i think that that's completely  pointless i think the last point i'll  mention for now  is embracing the worst case scenario  this case was that i would fail an exam  or that i would have to quit university  so realistically my biggest fear was i  would fail an exam  and well now that seems absolutely  ridiculous to me it's  not a problem to purely an exam it  doesn't mean a thing there's retakes  there's so many systems in place to make  sure that you succeed  a lot more than there are if you fail i  don't think there's anyone who really  who got accepted into medical school  really really wanted to do it  and just couldn't have to be kicked out  for academic reasons  so i think that there's no point in  stressing about this i know it sounds  very very easy on paper but this was one  of those cases that changed for me so  much through talking to another doctor  and he asked me how many exams had  failed in medical school  and i looked at him saying excuse me  of course none it was it was ridiculous  to me to think that i could fail an exam  and he laughed and said oh he failed at  least one exam a year  and this was a surgeon a qualified  surgeon a very good one also  um from what others say um so  that completely shifted my mindset and  thinking i'm being so ridiculous because  i  at that stage could only dream of  becoming a surgeon and to think that the  surgeon just very light-heartedly said  what medical school that's that's a joke  like if you fail it doesn't mean a thing  you're going you're probably going to  fail something and if you don't i mean  it's it's not a big plus really no one's  going to give you a badge for not  failing  and if you're having so much anxiety  around it it's i think completely  pointless  so i think wondering what is the worst  case scenario  realistically you do all this work and  you try so hard and  you sit the exam and you don't pass it  for some reason okay  then you just wait a few weeks or months  and set it later  and pass then and move on and in the  grand scheme of things  15 years later when you're a surgeon or  a gp or whatever you want to do  you're not even going to remember that  and it's really not going to matter  so i think that my advice would be to  look at these older doctors  and to realize how little they care  right now about their medical school  because they can really help put things  in perspective for you if you have the  opportunity to be around one who's very  generous with their time  just let them know about how you're  feeling be very open and say that i  think that everyone else  is so much better than me and i'm kind  of stressed that  perhaps i'm not good enough and i think  that they can really really help with  putting things in perspective for you by  nature human beings are not very good at  looking at the bigger picture  across large time scales but in this  case  if you can actually manage to do that  even once in a while  to realize that in the grand scheme of  things your silly little anatomy exam is  really not going to matter  and my stupid final is just another  like tick box exercise that i need to do  along the whole scale of my existence so  it doesn't really matter at all  and i think that perspective really  really changes things if  on the other hand your biggest fear is  realizing that this actually isn't for  you  then you're so lucky because the sooner  you realize that if you do get to  realize that in your first year second  year third year six years after you've  become a doctor  it's fine you can move on as soon as  possible it's not the single thing to do  in the world and it's probably not the  best thing to do in the world and  definitely not the best thing to do in  the world for everyone  so the sooner you realize that i think  take it as a gift  and do what you must with it continue or  move on or just depending on your  situation  so just to summarize the first point  would be that there's not enough  evidence that can be given  to get rid of that uncertainty and there  never will be so the sooner you get used  to that feeling of uncertainty  and see it in a positive way or  completely ignore it  the happier you will be the second point  would be to distinguish between  competitiveness that you can change  and competitiveness that you cannot the  things that you cannot you should  completely ignore  they don't matter they're just taking  away your energy the sooner you can get  used to this habit  the happier you will be my third point  is to  think of the worst case scenario which  are you exactly scared of  and how actually bad would that coming  true be  obvious i've put things in perspective  by reaching out to students in years  above you  or doctors even consultants the older  the better  they can really really help you realize  that you're freaking out for absolutely  no reason  and lastly if you're only getting  negative feelings around medicine and  you're feeling it's not right for you  that's absolutely fine i think that  there's no point in being scared that it  is not the right thing for you  the sooner you realize that the better i  am not particularly tied to medicine  i do it as long as i enjoy it at some  point i feel that it's not right for me  i'm happy to move on i find this kind of  attitude a lot more  calming and it removes a lot of that  pressure who made it so far honestly  thank you so much for spending this time  with me  i honestly hope from the bottom of my  heart that anything  that i said helped you at all please  feel free to reach out to me either in  the comments below or my instagram page  which is somewhere on my channel i'm  very very happy to talk  and hopefully give you some more advice  if you'd like some  i'll try to link some good videos on  imposter syndrome by people who are a  lot more experienced and a lot smarter  than me below if you would like to look  at those too  as always thank you so much i hope you  have a wonderful rest of your day  be kind to yourself and others and don't  believe everything you think thanks  bye

